        <td>3</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Funds from Sponsors/Personal Funds</td>
        <td>4</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Estimated International Student Expenses</td>
        <td>4</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Application for Form I-20</td>
        <td>6</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>International Transfer Students</td>
        <td>6</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Reason for I-20 Request</td>
        <td>7</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Affidavit of Support</td>
        <td>7</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Affidavit of Sponsor Providing Free Room and Board</td>
        <td>8</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>When and How will you receive Form I-20?</td>
        <td>9</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td colspan="2" style="text-align: center; font-weight: bold;">WHAT IS A FORM I-20 AND WHY DO YOU NEED ONE?</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3 style="margin-top: 40px;">Form I-20 Overview</h3>
  <p>Form I-20 is an official document that tells the U.S. government that <span data-field="university_name">{{ university_name }}</span> has determined that you are eligible for F-1 Student Status. It certifies that (1) you are or expect to be a "bona fide" student; (2) you meet our admissions requirements; (3) you will pursue a full course of study; (4) you provided us documents that indicate that you have enough money to study and live in the United States without working illegally or experiencing serious financial difficulties.</p>
  <p>You need a Form I-20 to obtain an F-1 student visa or status, or to keep lawful F-1 status when transferring or changing schools within the United States.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top: 30px;">DOES EVERYONE NEED A FORM I-20?</h3>
  <p>NO, some international students do not need a Form I-20.</p>
  <p>J-1 students and F-2 dependents visa holders must obtain a Form I-20 and apply for a change of status to F-1 student before pursuing a course of study that is not vocational or recreational in nature.</p>
  <p>If you are maintaining another nonimmigrant status in the U.S., you do not have an I-20 and may attend school full or part time. Dependent children in E, H, I, J, L, M, N, O, P, R, or S status need to change status after their 21st birthday or if they marry.</p>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <h3 style="margin-top: 30px;">REQUIREMENTS FOR OBTAINING YOUR FORM I-20</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>You must be a "bona fide" student, accepted to a full-time program of study.</li>
    <li>As required by United States law, you must prove that you can support the costs of living and studying in the U.S. for each year of your program of study.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3 style="margin-top: 30px;">DO NOT EXPECT TO WORK IN THE U.S. TO HELP MEET YOUR ANNUAL COSTS!</h3>
  <p>On-campus employment is limited and competitive; off-campus employment is strictly controlled by U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services (USCIS). Additionally, the USC IS team does not guide students through the process of obtaining a social security number; that process, when applicable, is the student's responsibility.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Carefully review the university's estimated annual financial requirements for international students and compute your total expenses. This is the amount that you must prove that you can provide for every year of your program of study (estimate a minimum of four years for a Bachelor's Degree; 2 years for a Master's Degree).</li>
    <li>These requirements are not negotiable. They represent a modest average budget that does not include luxuries. We strongly recommend that you budget at least 10% more if possible. We advise that students and their families look closely at the costs of living and studying in the U.S. and make careful plans to be sure your needs will be met.</li>
    <li>Expect modest annual increases in tuition and living costs.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>Read and follow these instructions very carefully.</strong> You cannot complete this process without following them step-by-step. We may ask you to provide more evidence of your financial ability; the U.S. government requires that we have confirmed, to the best of our ability, that you will have enough financial support to cover the full costs of your stay in the U.S.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top: 40px;">INFORMATION REGARDING FINANCIAL DOCUMENTS</h3>
  <p><span data-field="university_name">{{ university_name }}</span> follows strict guidelines regarding financial documentation for two reasons:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>We try to be as thorough as the U.S. embassy or consulate and the Department of Homeland Security Centers. Our purpose is to be sure that <span data-field="university_name">{{ university_name }}</span> students are not denied immigration applications because of financial questions. In order to be fair, we require all students to meet the same standards.</li>
    <li>Insufficient funds or the inability to access funds may result in serious problems for students. We advise that families be knowledgeable regarding the real costs of studying and living in New York [Long Island] and that they are as certain as possible that the student's financial needs can be satisfied.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 style="margin-top: 30px;">General Information:</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Sponsors should only promise what they can truly provide for your educational costs.</li>
    <li>Bank statements must contain actual U.S. dollar amounts.</li>
    <li>Documents must be current. We will not accept documents that are more than two months old.</li>
    <li>Copies or faxes, not originals, should be provided. You will present the originals at the embassy when applying for an F-1 visa.</li>
    <li>Documents must be in English.</li>
    <li>Every dollar amount being promised must be proven with supporting documentation.</li>
    <li>Scholarships and grants may be used to qualify for an I-20.</li>
    <li>Funds from sponsors and your personal funds may be combined. Any combination of support is acceptable. For example, (a) you can have a sponsor or sponsors for financial support; (b) you can have another to provide you with room and board; (c) you can...</li>
  </ol>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <p>Sponsor yourself. Be sure that all financial support equals the total amount of the budget in the <span data-field="estimated_expenses_section">{{ estimated_expenses_section }}</span> section below. Please make sure that you provide us with evidence to go with each form of support.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top: 30px;">SPONSORS PROVIDING FUNDING FOR YOUR EDUCATION</h3>
  <p>These funds may be provided by members of your family, friends, and/or organizations, including <span data-field="university_name">{{ university_name }}</span>, who have agreed to sponsor you in the form of cash, scholarships, and/or housing and board:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Your sponsor(s) must provide proof of their ability to make stated funds available to you. They must give you, in addition to the affidavit of support and bank statements, income tax statements or a letter from an employer verifying their annual salary or at least 3 months of pay stubs.</li>
    <li>A sponsor can agree to provide housing and food (room and board), for which the cash value is listed below, and the same or a different sponsor can promise you the remainder of the cost of attendance.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>TO SPONSORS (Please Read Carefully):</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Complete the attached affidavit of support.</li>
    <li>Depending on the support you are providing, attach proof of your financial ability to the Affidavit form: bank statements, along with income tax copies; letters of employment and/or salary stubs; copies of the apartment lease or the deed to your home if you are promising room and board.</li>
    <li>Return all documents to the student as soon as possible to be submitted as part of the packet.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 style="margin-top: 30px;">PERSONAL FUNDS</h3>
  <p>These are funds from your own resources. Please provide us with a completed Affidavit of Support, unless all financial documents appear in your name. The money can be in U.S. banks or abroad but it is recommended that you have at least half in U.S. banks so that it is available for you to use immediately.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top: 30px;">ESTIMATED ANNUAL INTERNATIONAL STUDENT EXPENSES</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th></th>
        <th>Undergraduate Programs</th>
        <th>Graduate Programs</th>
        <th>Graduate - MFA</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
